10-5-26 12:14am  🔮 GPT
Oh! 😂 Konami is a Japanese video-game company.
The famous Konami Code comes from one of their games. The sequence is:
↑ ↑ ↓ ↓ ← → ← → B A
It was first used by programmer Kazuhisa Hashimoto in the 1986 arcade/NES game Gradius. He put it in as a cheat/testing shortcut, and it eventually became famous far beyond Konami games.
Konami is the company behind games/franchises such as Metal Gear, Castlevania, Contra, Silent Hill, Dance Dance Revolution, and Gradius.
So when we say America.gov uses the “Konami Code,” we're saying its developers deliberately borrowed a 40-year-old video-game cultural reference and put the sequence into their government website.
Which, honestly, makes the choice even funnier:
1986 Konami cheat code → 2026 U.S. federal website. 😆
And now you know why everyone keeps calling it “the Konami code.”

